        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Wo? e Wohin?: «in» and «to»",
        "explicacao": "With «in», German distinguishes WHERE you are (Wo?) from WHERE TO you are going (Wohin?). Wo? uses the DATIVE: masculine and neuter «im» (in dem), feminine «in der». Wohin? uses the ACCUSATIVE: masculine «in den», feminine «in die», neuter «ins» (in das). To go to a place there is also «zu» (always dative): zum (zu dem) Bahnhof, zur (zu der) Post; and «nach» for cities and countries: nach Berlin. The full cases come in lessons 11 to 13; for now, memorise the table.",
        "comparacao": "In English the preposition changes («in the park», «to the park» or «into the park»). In German the preposition is the same («in»), and it is the article that changes.",
        "tabela": [
          {
            "gender": "masculine",
            "wo": "im Park",
            "wohin": "in den Park",
            "zu": "zum Park"
          },
          {
